course: dictionary
lesson: 13278
title: Governance: who runs things, and how
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Governance is a big, formal word for how something is run. It means the way a country, a club, a company or a school is controlled, and who makes the decisions and how they are made.
explain: «Good governance means the leaders are fair, honest and open about their decisions.» The clues are fair, honest and open. They tell you governance is about how the people in charge behave when they run things.
explain: «The school governors meet each term to check the school's governance.» Governors are the people who help run a school, so governance is the running and control of the school. Plain words for governance are running, management and rule.
fact: Governance means the way something is run and controlled.
fact: It includes who makes the decisions, and how those decisions are made.
fact: «Good governance» means fair, honest and open running of a country or company.
fact: «School governance» is how a school is run, and school governors look after it.
fact: «Governance» is a stiff, formal word; you can often say running, management or rule instead.
exercise: «Good governance means decisions are fair, honest and open.» What does governance mean here? | A school trip | A game played in the playground | A kind of weather | *The way a country or organisation is run and controlled
exercise: «The school governors look after the school's governance.» Who looks after a school's governance? | The head of the football team | *The school governors | The school cook | The bus driver
exercise: «The club's governance is fair.» Which plain word could replace governance here? | Breakfast | Colour | *Management | Sleeping
test: «The council's governance kept the town clean and safe.» What does governance mean here? | *The way the town is run and controlled | A fast bus | A large, old building | A kind of cleaning spray
test: Which words best describe good governance? | *Fair, honest and open running of a country or company | Short and rude replies | Very hungry and tired | Loud and noisy shouting in the street
exam: Which sentence uses governance correctly? | The rain has governance today | I ate my governance for lunch | *Good governance means leaders make fair, honest decisions | The cat had good governance and slept all day
